# Content Security Policy.
#
# Report-Only is deliberate, and removing it would break the app. Measured by
# serving this exact policy as enforcing and driving the real surfaces:
#
#   script-src | eval          x4  <- image compression dies here
#   script-src-elem | inline   x2
#   script-src-attr | inline   x2
#
# The `eval` is not ours. There is no `eval(` or `new Function(` anywhere in
# src/; it comes from the Emscripten glue shipped with the WASM engines
# (ImageMagick, FFmpeg and friends), which build functions from strings at
# init. `wasm-unsafe-eval` permits compiling WebAssembly but not that, so an
# enforcing policy leaves a compression run hanging forever rather than failing
# visibly. Enforcing it would need `unsafe-eval`, which gives away most of what
# the policy is for.
#
# So this documents an intent the app does not yet meet, and a strict policy is
# not reachable while these engines ship. Do not flip it to
# `Content-Security-Policy` expecting it to hold as written.
#
# There is still a worthwhile version, if anyone wants it. Note the ordering
# trap: this policy grants no `unsafe-inline` either, so fixing the eval alone
# would not be enough. Both have to go together.
#
#   DONE - inline event handlers. The two `script-src-attr` violations were the
#   async-CSS `onload="this.onload=null;this.rel='stylesheet'"` attributes the
#   build injected. They now carry `data-async-css` and are flipped by
#   /async-css.js. 8 violations -> 6.
#
#   DONE - inline scripts. The `script-src_elem` pair is covered by the
#   `__CSP_SCRIPT_HASHES__` placeholder below, which the `csp-hashes` plugin in
#   vite.config.js replaces at build time with a sha256 per inline script,
#   computed from the final files in dist/ so a hash can never disagree with the
#   bytes that ship. Moving those blocks to files was considered and rejected:
#   the first sets `.dark` before the first paint, so an external script adds a
#   round trip and can reintroduce the white flash it exists to prevent.
#   6 violations -> 4.
#
#   REMAINING - one decision. All four survivors are `eval`. Enforcing needs
#   `unsafe-eval` added, or those three libraries dropped or replaced.
#
# The eval is three ordinary JS libraries rather than the WASM engines:
# flptojson (ts-flp), font, and turbowarp. Measured at
# assets/flptojson-*.js, assets/font-*.js and assets/turbowarp-*.js.
#
# That trades an accurate description for actual enforcement of the four
# directives that can hold - connect-src, object-src, frame-ancestors and
# base-uri - none of which enforce anything today.
#
# `connect-src 'self'` is exact and worth keeping exact: nothing in the shipped
# bundle contacts any external host, which is what makes "your files never
# leave your device" checkable rather than a promise. It previously allowed
# https://cdn.sheetjs.com, which nothing ever called - xlsx is bundled at
# install time - so the allowance was granting reach the app does not use.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' __CSP_SCRIPT_HASHES__; worker-src 'self' blob:; img-src 'self' blob: data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
